#: src/installing.md:1
msgid "Installing Redox"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:3
msgid ""
"Redox OS provides an installer to facilitate installation from Redox OS "
"itself or Linux. **Please back up your system** before attempting to "
"install. Note that at this time (Release 0.9.0), you can't install onto a "
"USB device or use a USB device for your Redox filesystem, but you can "
"install from it."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:5
msgid "Starting the Installer from Redox OS Livedisk"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:7
msgid ""
"The installer is usually pre-installed on Redox OS. After starting your "
"Redox OS _livedisk_ system from a USB device or CD/DVD, log in as the user "
"named `user` with an empty password, click on the Redox OS icon in the "
"Orbital bottom bar to open the app menu, then open the \"System\" app "
"category and click on the \"Redox Installer\" app."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:9
msgid ""
"Or, if you want to launch it from the terminal, run the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:15
msgid ""
"If you are using the `server` variant or want to use the TUI interface, open "
"a terminal window and type:"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:21
msgid ""
"At the moment, the GUI installer is more capable than the TUI version, but "
"the TUI one will be updated."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:23
msgid ""
"In the TUI version, if Redox recognizes your device, it will prompt you to "
"select a device to install on. Choose carefully, as it will erase all the "
"data on that device. Note that if your device is not recognized, it may "
"offer you the option to install on `disk/live` (the in-memory _livedisk_). "
"Don't do this, as it will crash Redox."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:25
msgid ""
"The TUI version also prompts for a `redoxfs password`. This is for an "
"encrypted filesystem. Leave the password empty and press Enter if you don't "
"need filesystem encryption."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:27
msgid "Starting the Installer from Linux"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:29
msgid ""
"At the moment, the GUI installer is capable of installation from Linux. "
"However, the binary is not distributed; you need to compile and install it "
"using `cargo`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:35
msgid ""
"The GUI installer is using `libcosmic`, so it will only work using Wayland. "
"After compiling it, launch it from terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:41
msgid ""
"Depending on how you will install Redox OS, you might want to run it with "
"`sudo`."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:43
msgid "GUI Installer Options"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:45
msgid ""
"The GUI installer is capable of many installation modes. This section "
"explains each step option and its implications in detail:"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:47
msgid "1. Where do you want to install Redox OS?"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:49
msgid ""
"This section asks about the general scenario where you want to install Redox "
"OS:"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:51
msgid "**Whole Drive**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:53
msgid ""
"Use the whole storage device for Redox OS. It will erase all data on that "
"device. The next section is [\\#2](#2-choose-a-drive)."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:55
msgid "**Specific Partition**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:57
msgid ""
"Use a storage device partition for Redox OS. It will erase all data in that "
"partition, but leave every other partition intact. It is the option that you "
"should use if you want to dual-boot with your current Linux installation. "
"The next section is [\\#3](#3-choose-a-main-partition)."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:59
msgid "**Image File**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:61
msgid ""
"Write to a new image file. This option will create an image disk file "
"similar to the one that's hosted on the Redox OS download page. The next "
"section is [\\#4](#4-configure-image-file)."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:64
msgid "2. Choose a drive"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:66
msgid ""
"This section lets you choose which storage device you want to install into. "
"When chosen, the disk will be erased, then formatted as GPT with three "
"partitions:"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:68
msgid "First, a BIOS boot partition for boot using legacy BIOS."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:69
msgid "Second, a 2MB FAT filesystem for UEFI bootloader."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:70
msgid "Last, a RedoxFS partition that occupies the rest of the disk."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:72
msgid ""
"This layout is not configurable. If you wish to do so, format your disk "
"using `GParted` or a similar tool, then go back and choose **Specific "
"Partition**."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:74
msgid "_Never choose the disk that's currently active_."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:76
msgid "3. Choose a main partition"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:78
msgid ""
"This section lets you choose which partition you want to use as the main "
"RedoxFS partition for Redox OS. This will erase the data for that partition "
"but leave everything else untouched."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:80
msgid ""
"The installer is not capable of doing partitioning. If you want to do so, "
"use another tool such as `GParted` and set up an empty partition for "
"RedoxFS, then click **Reload** on this page after you're done partitioning."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:82
msgid ""
"Note that installing just for RedoxFS partition will not make it bootable; "
"hence, there's a second option to choose where to install the bootloader:"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:84
msgid "**Do not write**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:86
msgid ""
"This will not write any bootloader and leave the installed Redox OS in that "
"partition unbootable, because Redox OS requires a custom bootloader and "
"cannot be loaded by common bootloaders such as `GRUB`, `systemd-boot`, etc."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:88
msgid "**Dual-boot (systemd-boot)**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:90
msgid ""
"If `/boot/efi` is detected to contain `systemd-boot`, this option will be "
"visible. This option is not visible for Linux that boots using other than "
"`systemd-boot` such as `GRUB`."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:92
msgid "When chosen, it will write the UEFI bootloader to these paths:"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:94
msgid "`/boot/efi/EFI/redox/*.efi` : The bootloader EFI file"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:95
msgid ""
"`/boot/efi/loader/entries/redox.conf` : The bootloader entry in \\`systemd-"
"boot menu"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:97
msgid ""
"In some Linux distributions, the `systemd-boot` menu is not visible, and to "
"make it appear, the `timeout` option in `/boot/efi/loader/loader.conf` file. "
"See [systemd-boot loader.conf man page](https://man.archlinux.org/man/loader."
"conf.5#OPTIONS) for more information."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:99
msgid ""
"If you choose any partition, make sure it's not mounted. **Never choose the "
"partition that's currently mounted**."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:101
msgid "**Any partition in /dev/\\***"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:103
msgid ""
"This option will format the partition with the FAT filesystem containing "
"UEFI bootloader for Redox OS. The bootloader does not have to be on the same "
"disk as RedoxFS, as the bootloader will look to all drives that contain a "
"RedoxFS partition with the help of UEFI services."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:105
msgid ""
"This option is meant for installation to an external storage device, or for "
"chainloading to any bootloader that supports it, such as `GRUB`. In some "
"machines, the bootloader can also be directly bootable from BIOS boot device "
"menu or boot priority on firmware setup."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:107
msgid ""
"Note that this option only installs the UEFI bootloader. If your machine "
"only supports booting via BIOS, then your only option is to go back and "
"choose the **Whole Drive** option."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:111
msgid "If you do not see the `Next` button, try to maximize the window."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:113
msgid "4. Configure Image File"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:115
msgid ""
"This section contain options for installing Redox OS into a image file. This "
"section is particularly useful for emulating Redox in a virtual machine, or "
"for advanced installation methods later on."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:117
msgid "**Image Path**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:119
msgid ""
"This asks where to install the image file. In Linux, the `Browse` button is "
"visible to open a file dialog where the file will be saved."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:121
msgid ""
"You can also specify a path to a directory, and it will just install Redox "
"OS files in that directory, but this behavior is not recommended to be used."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:123
msgid "**Image Size**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:125
msgid ""
"Specify the image file size, which will also be the size of the main RedoxFS "
"partition for Redox OS."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:127
msgid "**Skip Partition?**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:129
msgid ""
"Checking this box will prevent the image file from being formatted as GPT "
"device; rather, it will become a raw RedoxFS partition (written directly to "
"storage device sectors). This is useful if you wanted only the RedoxFS "
"format, which can then be used for `dd` into `/dev/partition` for example, "
"or boot via the `-cdrom` option in `QEMU`."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:131
msgid "**Write Bootloader EFI**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:133
msgid ""
"This asks where to install the bootloader file. The EFI file is needed to "
"boot the Redox OS disk later on. It is a PE executable that is usually "
"placed in `/EFI/BOOT/*.EFI` of a boot partition."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:136
msgid "5. Select System Profile"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:138
msgid ""
"After entering options from either Section 2, 3, or 4, this is the last "
"section before the actual install is performed."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:140
msgid "**Desktop**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:142
msgid ""
"This option chooses the Redox OS to be installed with desktop and server "
"components. The installation usually takes around 3 minutes (200 seconds)."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:144
msgid "**Server**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:146
msgid ""
"This option chooses the Redox OS to be installed with only server "
"components. Its installation usually takes around 1 minute and half (90 "
"seconds)."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:148
msgid "**Clone**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:150
msgid ""
"This option is only visible if you boot Redox OS to a live disk. It will "
"install an identical copy from the current Redox OS live disk memory."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:152
msgid "**Install as Live Disk**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:154
msgid ""
"This option will make the bootloader install with live disk mode being "
"chosen as the default."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:156
msgid "**Enable disk encryption password**"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:158
msgid ""
"You can enter a password for main RedoxFS partition encryption. It is whole-"
"disk encryption; the bootloader will ask for the password later to boot "
"Redox."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:161
msgid "6. Installation Progress"
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:163
msgid ""
"The installation is then performed and usually will finish within minutes. "
"Unfortunately, for the GUI installer, the progress is in the terminal where "
"it was launched, instead of being reported in the GUI."
msgstr ""

#: src/installing.md:165
msgid ""
"Once the installation completes, you can restart your computer to boot into "
"Redox!"
msgstr ""
